    "q-32617": {
      "dom_id": "step-q32617",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "Given that \n$B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 \\\\ 7 & 20\\end{aligned}]$ \nhas the LU decomposition $B=LU$, then ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{12}=$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $B$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $B$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 \\\\ 7 & 20\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{7}{3}){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{3} & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot B \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}B=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}B & =U \\\\ B & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ B & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{3} & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ B & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{7}{3} & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=\\frac{7}{3},{u}_{12}=9,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{12}=21.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
