    "q-32531": {
      "dom_id": "step-q32531",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "Given that \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 10\\end{aligned}]$ has the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{5}{2}){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=\\frac{5}{2},{u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=\\frac{5}{2}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
